"NeuSpell: A Neural Spelling Correction Toolkit (https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-demos.21.pdf)\nciteturn58view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view3\",\"lineno\":152}); Total lines: 459\nL143@P2: During inference, we first replace UNK predic\u0002tions with their corresponding input words and then\nL144@P2: evaluate the results. We evaluate models for accu\u0002racy (percentage of correct words among all words)\nL145@P2: and word correction rate (percentage of misspelt to\u0002kens corrected). We use AllenNLP6\nL146@P2: and Hugging\u0002face7\nL147@P2: libraries to use ELMo and BERT respectively.\nL148@P2: All neural models in our toolkit are implemented\nL149@P2: using the Pytorch library (Paszke et al., 2017), and\nL150@P2: are compatible to run on both CPU and GPU en\u0002vironments. Performance of different models are\nL151@P2: presented in Table 1.\nL152@P2: 3 Synthetic Training Datasets\nL153@P2: Due to scarcity of available parallel data for\nL154@P2: spelling correction, we noise sentences to gener\u0002ate misspelt-correct sentence pairs. We use 1.6M\nL155@P2: sentences from the one billion word benchmark\nL156@P2: (Chelba et al., 2013) dataset as our clean corpus.\nL157@P2: Using different noising strategies from existing lit\u0002erature, we noise ∼20% of the tokens in the clean\nL158@P2: corpus by injecting spelling mistakes in each sen\u0002tence. Below, we briefly describe these strategies.\nL159@P2: RANDOM: Following Sakaguchi et al. (2016),\nL160@P2: this noising strategy involves four character-level\nL161@P2: operations: permute, delete, insert and replace. We\nL162@P2: manipulate only the internal characters of a word.\nL163@P2: The permute operation jumbles a pair of consecu\u0002tive characters, delete operation randomly deletes\nL164@P2: one of the characters, insert operation randomly\nL165@P2: inserts an alphabet and replace operation swaps a\nL166@P2: character with a randomly selected alphabet. For\nL167@P2: every word in the clean corpus, we select one of\nL168@P2: the four operations with 0.1 probability each. We\nL169@P2: do not modify words of length three or smaller.\nL170@P2: WORD: Inspired from Belinkov and Bisk (2017),\nL171@P2: we swap a word with its noised counterpart from a\nL172@P2: pre-built lookup table. We collect 109K misspelt\u0002correct word pairs for 17K popular English words\nL173@P2: from a variety of public sources.8\nL174@P2: For every word in the clean corpus, we replace it\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInstruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2311.07911v1)\nciteturn58view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn49view4\",\"lineno\":95}); Total lines: 2099\nL94: It would be difficult to say if a tested model is good at following a particular instruction or if it is simply good at following a certain phrasing of the instruction.\nL95: We alleviate these problems by creating our prompts through four steps. First, we generate a set of base prompts with one to three randomly selected verifiable instructions appended to the end of each prompt. Then, we use few-shot prompting to identify illogical prompts and remove them. As the third step, we apply another few-shot prompting based approach to rephrase each prompt, to increase the diversity of phrasing. Finally, we manually check and edit the rephrased prompts one by one.\nL96: #### 2.2 IFEval metrics\nL97: \nL98: For a given response $resp$ and a verifiable instruction $inst$, we define the function that verifies if the instruction is followed or not as:\nL99: \nL100:  | $$\\textrm{is{\\_}followed}(resp,inst)=\\begin{cases}\\text{True},&\\text{if instruction is followed}.\\\\\nL101: \\text{False},&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$  |  | (1)\nL102: \nL103: We use Equation cite48†1 to compute the instruction following accuracy, and refer to it as the strict metric.\nL104: Even though we can verify if an instruction is followed using simple heuristics and programming, we found that there are still false negatives. For example, for a given verifiable instruction of “end your email with: P.S. I do like the cake”, a language model may follow the instruction by ending the email with “P.S. **I do like the cake**” which has markdown tags (** indicates the bold text). If we simply check the string match of “P.S. I do like the cake”, we will miss-classify it as not-followed.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOptimizing LLM Inference: Fluid-Guided Online Scheduling with Memory Constraints (https://arxiv.org/html/2504.11320v4)\nciteturn58view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view0\",\"lineno\":1671}); Total lines: 1735\nL1669: Figure cite225†16 reports the comparison across arrival rates. At low arrival rates, waiting introduces a modest latency cost: requests may wait to accumulate even when the server has enough capacity to process them immediately. This cost shrinks as the arrival rate increases. Near the overload boundary, waiting becomes useful because it regulates the timing at which work enters service, reducing latency relative to the threshold-only variant.\nL1670: Once the system is overloaded, the upper bound becomes the dominant control, and the two variants again behave similarly.\nL1671: Figure 16: Effect of waiting on the single-type p512d20 Vidur workload. At each arrival rate, full WAIT and the threshold-only variant use the same parameter setting; the comparison only changes whether service waits for enough requests to accumulate before scheduling.\nL1672: ### H.3 Real-GPU validation\nL1673: We complement the Vidur experiments in Section cite26†6 with measurements on a physical NVIDIA A100 80 GB serving Llama-2-7B. These runs serve two purposes. First, direct GPU measurements calibrate the iteration-time model used by Vidur. Second, we implement WAIT’s admission logic using SGLang 0.5.7, an open-source LLM serving framework (cite226†SGLang Team, 2024 ), and test the resulting scheduler in end-to-end GPU runs.\nL1674: We report three comparisons: iteration-time measurements against Vidur’s prediction, a single-type GPU workload corresponding to Section cite27†6.1 , and a real-data GPU workload corresponding to Section cite32†6.2 .\nL1675: ##### Simulator vs GPU.\nL1676: To calibrate the simulator in the operating region used in Section cite26†6 , we compare Vidur’s iteration-time predictions with direct measurements on the same A100 80 GB GPU running Llama-2-7B under the SGLang baseline scheduler. Vidur’s raw Llama-2-7B attention profiling covers batch sizes up to $128$. Within this profiled range, the validation uses batch sizes $B\\in\\{1,2,4,\\ldots,128\\}$ with prefill length $256$ and decode length $20$; we also test extrapolation to $B=256$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn58view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn56view3\",\"pattern\":\"threshold\"}); Total lines: 1\nNo matching text found for \"threshold\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/file/724be4472168f31ba1c9ac630f15dec8-Paper-Conference.pdf)\nciteturn58view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn54view0\",\"pattern\":\"compressed finite\"}); Total lines: 1640\nL10@P0: require multiple generation calls, advanced prompting techniques, control flow, and\nL11@P0: structured inputs/outputs. However, efficient systems are lacking for programming\nL12@P0: and executing these applications. We introduce SGLang, a system for efficient\nL13@P0: execution of complex language model programs. SGLang consists of a frontend\nL14@P0: language and a runtime. The frontend simplifies programming with primitives for\nL15@P0: generation and parallelism control. The runtime accelerates execution with novel\nL16@P0: optimizations like RadixAttention for KV cache reuse and compressed finite state\nL17@P0: machines for faster structured output decoding. Experiments show that SGLang\nL18@P0: achieves up to 6.4× higher throughput compared to state-of-the-art inference\nL19@P0: systems on various large language and multi-modal models on tasks including\nL20@P0: agent control, logical reasoning, few-shot learning benchmarks, JSON decoding,\nL21@P0: retrieval-augmented generation pipelines, and multi-turn chat. The code is publicly\nL22@P0: available at https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang.\nL23@P0: 1 Introduction\nL24@P0: Recent increases in the capabilities of LLMs have broadened their utility, enabling them to tackle a\nL25@P0: wider range of general tasks and act as autonomous agents [35, 6, 36, 52, 46]. In such applications,\nL26@P0: LLMs engage in multi-round planning, reasoning, and interaction with external environments. This\nL27@P0: is accomplished through tool usage [41, 38], multiple input modalities [47, 2], and a wide range of\nL28@P0: prompting techniques [30], like few-shot learning [5], self-consistency [53], skeleton-of-thought [33],\nL29@P0: and tree-of-thought [56]. All of these new use cases require multiple, often dependent, LLM\nL30@P0: generation calls, showing a trend of using multi-call structures to complete complex tasks [57, 21].\nL31@P0: The emergence of these patterns signifies a shift in our interaction with LLMs, moving from simple\nL32@P0: chatting to a more sophisticated form of programmatic usage of LLMs, which means using a program\nL33@P0: to schedule and control the generation processes of LLMs. We refer to these programs as \"Language\nL34@P0: Model Programs\" (LM Programs) [4, 20]. The advanced prompting techniques and agentic workflow\nL35@P0: mentioned above fall within the scope of LM programs. There are two common properties of LM\nL36@P0: programs: (1) LM programs typically contain multiple LLM calls interspersed with control flow.\nL37@P0: This is needed to complete complex tasks and improve overall quality. (2) LM programs receive\nL38@P0: structured inputs and produce structured outputs. This is needed to enable the composition of LM\nL39@P0: programs and to integrate LM programs into existing software systems.\nL40@P0: ∗Equal contribution.\nL41@P0-1: 38th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024).Interpreter\nL42@P1: SGLang Client (Frontend)\nL43@P1: Language primitives (Sec. 2)\nL44@P1: SGLang Runtime (Backend)\nL45@P1: Optimizations: RadixAttention (Sec. 3), Compressed finite\nL46@P1: state machines (Sec. 4), API speculative execution (Sec. 5)\nL47@P1: Figure 1: System architecture: An interpreter executes language primitives with optimized runtime.\nL48@P1: Despite the widespread use of LM programs, current systems for expressing and executing them\nL49@P1: remain inefficient. We identify two primary challenges associated with the efficient use of LM pro\u0002grams: First, programming LM programs is tedious and difficult due to the non-deterministic nature of\nL50@P1: LLMs. Developing an LM program often requires extensive string manipulation, experimental tuning\nL51@P1: of prompts, brittle output parsing, handling multiple input modalities, and implementing parallelism\nL52@P1: mechanisms. This complexity significantly reduces the readability of even simple programs (Sec. 2).\nL53@P1: Secondly and importantly, executing LM programs is inefficient due to redundant computation\nL54@P1: and memory usage. State-of-the-art inference engines (e.g., vLLM [23], TGI [16], and TensorRT\u0002LLM [34]), have been optimized to reduce latency and improve throughput without direct knowledge\nL55@P1: of the workload. This makes these systems general and robust but also results in significant ineffi\u0002ciencies for any given workload. A prominent example is the reuse of the Key-Value (KV) cache\nL56@P1: (Sec. 3). The KV cache consists of reusable intermediate tensors that are essential for generative\nL57@P1: inference. During typical batch executions of LM programs, numerous opportunities exist to reuse\nL58@P1: the KV cache across multiple different LLM calls that share a common prefix. However, current\nL59@P1: systems lack effective mechanisms to facilitate this reuse, resulting in unnecessary computations and\nL60@P1: wasted memory. Another example is constrained decoding for structured outputs (e.g., JSON mode),\nL61@P1: where the output of LLMs is restricted to follow specific grammatical rules defined by a regular\nL62@P1: expression (Sec. 4). Under these constraints, multiple tokens can often be decoded once. However,\nL63@P1: existing systems only decode one token at a time, leading to suboptimal decoding speeds.\nL74@P1: asynchronous execution, ensuring proper control over synchronization and intra-program parallelism.\nL75@P1: Additionally, SGLang program can be traced and compiled for more optimizations.\nL76@P1: On the runtime side, we propose several novel optimizations to accelerate the execution of SGLang\nL77@P1: programs. The first technique, RadixAttention, enables the automatic reuse of the KV cache across\nL78@P1: multiple generation calls. In existing inference engines, the KV cache of a request is discarded\nL79@P1: after processing is completed, preventing the KV cache from being reused across multiple calls and\nL80@P1: significantly slowing down the execution. Instead, our system maintains an LRU cache of the KV\nL81@P1: cache for all requests within a radix tree. This approach manages the KV cache as a traditional cache\nL82@P1: and uses a radix tree for efficient matching, insertion, and eviction. It allows the runtime to handle\nL83@P1: various reuse patterns with a cache-aware scheduling policy efficiently. The second technique is a\nL84@P1: compressed finite state machine, which enables faster constrained decoding for structured outputs.\nL85@P1: Existing systems follow the constraints only for the next token by masking probabilities of disallowed\nL86@P1: tokens, making them able to decode only one token at a time. Instead, our system analyzes the\nL87@P1: constraints and builds a compressed finite-state machine to represent the constraint. This approach\nL88@P1: compresses a multi-token path into a single-step path whenever possible, allowing the decoding of\nL89@P1: multiple tokens at once to achieve faster decoding speed. Lastly, SGLang also supports API-only\nL90@P1: models like OpenAI’s GPT-4, and we introduce the third technique, API speculative execution, to\nL91@P1: optimize multi-call programs for API-only models.\nL92@P1: Using SGLang, we implemented various LLM applications, including agent control, logical reasoning,\nL93@P1: few-shot learning benchmarks, JSON decoding, retrieval-augmented generation pipelines, multi\u0002turn chat, and multi-modality processing. We tested the performance on models including Llama\u00027B/70B [49], Mistral-8x7B [17], LLaVA-v1.5-7B (image) [28], and LLaVA-NeXT-34B (video) [62]\nL94@P1: on NVIDIA A10G and A100 GPUs. Experimental results show that SGLang achieves up to 6.4×\nL95@P1: higher throughput across a wide range of workloads, models, and hardware setups, compared to\nL395@P5: to starvation. We leave its integration with other fair scheduling methods [42] as future work.\nL396@P5: Distributed Cases. RadixAttention can be extended to multiple GPUs. For tensor parallelism, each\nL397@P5: GPU maintains a sharded KV cache. There is no need for additional synchronization because the tree\nL398@P5: operations are the same. Data parallelism with multiple workers is discussed in Sec. A.4 (Appendix).\nL399@P5: 4 Efficient Constrained Decoding with Compressed Finite State Machine\nL400@P5: In LM programs, users often want to constrain the model’s output to follow specific formats, such\nL401@P5: as JSON schemas. This can improve controllability and robustness, and make the output easier to\nL402@P5: parse. SGLang offers a regex argument to enforce such constraints using regular expressions, which\nL403@P5: are expressive enough for many practical scenarios. Existing systems support this by converting a\nL404@P5: regular expression into a finite state machine (FSM) [54]. During decoding, they maintain the current\nL551@P7: SGLang parallelizes the generation calls within a single program and reuses the KV cache as much\nL552@P7: as possible. On JSON decoding, SGLang accelerates decoding by decoding multiple tokens at once\nL553@P7: with a compressed finite state machine. In multi-turn chat, SGLang reuses the KV cache of the chat\nL554@P7: history. The speedup is more noticeable for short outputs because KV cache reuse mostly helps\nL555@P7: reduce the prefix time. For long outputs, because there is not much sharing between different chat\nL556@P7: sessions and the decoding time dominates, there is almost no speedup. In the DSPy RAG pipeline,\nL557@P7: SGLang reuses the KV cache of the common context example. On these benchmarks, the cache hit\nL558@P7: rate ranges from 50% to 99%. Fig. 13 (Appendix) lists the achieved and optimal cache hit rates for all\nL559@P7: of them, showing that our cache-aware scheduling approaches 96% of the optimal hit rate on average.\nL560@P7: We exclude LMQL and Guidance from some of the last five benchmarks due to slow performance and\nL644@P8: Disabling parallelism and hints from the frontend interpreter also results in suboptimal runtime\nL645@P8: performance, highlighting the importance of co-designing the frontend language and runtime.\nL646@P8: Overhead of RadixAttention. We test the overhead of RadixAttention on a benchmark without any\nL647@P8: KV cache reuse opportunities. The benchmark measures throughput on the ShareGPT dataset. It\nL648@P8: takes 74.3 seconds to run 100 requests; however, the time used for managing the RadixAttention data\nL649@P8: structures is only 0.2 seconds, which is a negligible overhead of less than 0.3%. This is because the\nL650@P8: complexity of tree operations is linear and small. Thus, we can turn on RadixAttention by default.\nL651@P8: Effectiveness of the compressed finite state machine. We test the effectiveness of the compressed\nL652@P8: finite state machine and its components on the JSON decoding benchmark. Experimental results\nL653@P8: show that the compressed finite state machine increases the throughput by 1.6× because it can decode\nL654@P8: multiple tokens at once. In addition, we need to preprocess the state machine and reuse it for a batch\nL655@P8: of requests. Otherwise, redoing the preprocessing for each request makes the throughput 2.4× lower.\nL656@P8: 7 Related Work\nL657@P8: Various works have explored the reuse of the KV cache, and many of them are concurrent with our\nL658@P8: work. Uniquely, our RadixAttention first proposes treating the KV cache as a tree-based LRU cache.\nL659@P8: It is the first solution that supports multi-level sharing, cache-aware scheduling, frontend-runtime\nL660@P8: co-scheduling, and distributed cases. vLLM [23] and ChunkedAttention [58] explore some simple\nL661@P8: reuse cases (e.g., system prompt sharing) but do not cover multi-level tree-structured sharing or LRU\nL662@P8: caching. PromptCache [12] proposes the modular reuse of the KV cache beyond the prefix but can\nL663@P8: impact accuracy by up to a 43% drop. HydraGen [18], FlashInfer [59], and ChunkedAttention [58]\nL664@P8: focus on CUDA kernel optimizations and do not include the concept of an LRU cache. InferCept [1]\nL665@P8: and LLM-SQL [29] study KV cache reuse for specific applications such as interleaving with external\nL666@P8: API calls and relational databases, but they do not have our radix tree or cache-aware scheduling.\nL667@P8-9: 9Several LLM programming and agent frameworks exist, such as Guidance [13], LMQL [4],\nL668@P9: DSPy [20], LangChain [24], AutoGen [55], and LLM Compiler [21]. Guidance and LMQL are most\nL669@P9: similar to SGLang, and we compare them in Sec. 2. Our innovation lies in novel runtime optimizations\nL670@P9: for accelerating the proposed programming model. SGLang is compatible with other frameworks and\nL671@P9: can accelerate them (e.g., the DSPy example in our evaluation). Additionally, SGLang is compatible\nL672@P9: with many other common inference optimizations [60, 39, 3, 23, 59, 10, 26, 15, 19, 32, 31, 11].\nL673@P9: 8 Future Directions and Conclusion\nL674@P9: Future directions. Despite the progress made with SGLang, several limitations remain that reveal\nL675@P9: promising directions for future research. These include extending SGLang to support additional\nL676@P9: output modalities, adapting RadixAttention to operate across multiple levels of the memory hierarchy\nL677@P9: (e.g., DRAM, Disk) [43], enabling fuzzy semantic matching within RadixAttention, providing higher\u0002level primitives atop SGLang, fixing starvation in cache-aware scheduling [42], and enhancing the\nL678@P9: SGLang compiler to perform advanced static optimizations such as scheduling and memory planning.\nL679@P9: Conclusion. We introduce SGLang, a framework for efficient programming and executing structured\nL680@P9: language model programs. SGLang significantly improves the throughput and latency of complex\nL681@P9: LM programs through novel optimizations like RadixAttention, compressed finite state machines,\nL682@P9: and a language interpreter. It is a valuable tool for developing advanced prompting techniques and\nL683@P9: agent workflows. The source code is publicly available.\nL684@P9: Acknowledgement\nL685@P9: This project is supported by the Stanford Center for Automated Reasoning and gifts from Astronomer,\nL686@P9: Google, IBM, Intel, Lacework, Microsoft, Mohamed Bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence,\nL687@P9: Nexla, Samsung SDS, Uber, and VMware. Lianmin Zheng is supported by a Meta Ph.D. Fellowship.\nL688@P9: We thank Yuanhan Zhang and Bo Li for the LLaVA-NeXT (video) support.\nL689@P9: References\nL690@P9: [1] Reyna Abhyankar, Zijian He, Vikranth Srivatsa, Hao Zhang, and Yiying Zhang. Infercept:\nL691@P9: Efficient intercept support for augmented large language model inference. In Forty-first Interna\u0002tional Conference on Machine Learning, Vienna, Austria, July 2024.\nL692@P9: [2] Jean-Baptiste Alayrac, Jeff Donahue, Pauline Luc, Antoine Miech, Iain Barr, Yana Hasson,\nL693@P9: Karel Lenc, Arthur Mensch, Katherine Millican, Malcolm Reynolds, et al. Flamingo: a visual\nL694@P9: language model for few-shot learning. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems,\nL1046@P16: both the router and workers independently update their respective trees. Should an eviction occur\nL1047@P16: at a worker node, it commits this eviction to a queue, which the router then processes to update the\nL1048@P16: meta-tree during periods of low activity. We benchmarked this distributed configuration using four\nL1049@P16: workers and the MMLU dataset, observing that it achieves linear scaling and an optimal cache hit rate\nL1050@P16: with minimal overhead from this weakly consistent distributed cache design. There exists a trade-off\nL1051@P16: between maximizing data locality and parallel processing efficiency. Exploring advanced scheduling\nL1052@P16: policies to optimize this trade-off is designated as an area for future research. In addition, concurrent\nL1053@P16: work from Preble [45] studies data-parallel scheduling based on an early version of SGLang.\nL1054@P16: B Additional Details on Compressed Finite State Machine\nL1055@P16: This section discusses the background and implementation details of the compressed finite state\nL1056@P16: machine for faster constrained decoding. We aim for LLMs to follow a regular expression (regex),\nL1057@P16-17: 17which offers greater expressiveness and can be used to represent common formats such as JSON\nL1058@P17: schemas. To achieve this, we convert the regex into a Finite State Machine (FSM) to guide the\nL1059@P17: generation process during decoding [54]. An FSM is essentially a graph with nodes (states) and\nL1060@P17: edges (transitions with strings/characters). Starting from an initial state, each transition appends the\nL1061@P17: string on the edge to move to the next state, using a set of final states to conclude the process. This\nL1062@P17: mechanism guides an LLM’s decoding, filtering out invalid tokens based on the FSM’s current state\nL1063@P17: transitions, as illustrated in Fig. 10. The decoding might involve multiple transitions in the FSM\nL1064@P17: until reaching a final state.\nL1065@P17: § age\nL1066@P17: § Age\nL1067@P17: § hou\nL1068@P17: § 0\nL1069@P17: § 1\nL1070@P17: § fif\nL1071@P17: …\nL1072@P17: Decode + FSM\nL1073@P17: Constrained Decoding With Logits Mask\nL1074@P17: Decoding Status\nL1075@P17: Regular Expression\nL1076@P17: 0 \" 1\nL1077@P17: a\nL1078@P17: 2 g 3\nL1079@P17: e\nL1080@P17: 4 \" 6\nL1081@P17: [0-9]\nL1082@P17: 5\nL1083@P17: : [0-9]\nL1084@P17: 7\nL1085@P17: ,\nL1086@P17: Finite State Machine\nL1087@P17: Decoding Status\nL1088@P17: Decode + FSM\nL1089@P17: ✔\nL1090@P17: ✖\nL1091@P17: allowed next token\nL1092@P17: not allowed next token\nL1093@P17: ✔\nL1094@P17: ✔\nL1095@P17: ✖\nL1096@P17: ✔\nL1097@P17: ✖\nL1098@P17: ✖\nL1099@P17: Figure 10: Example of how regex is converted into FSM and how FSM guides the decoding process.\nL1100@P17: The challenge of constrained decoding arises from the fact that constraints are often expressed in\nL1101@P17: natural language formats, i.e., regex is depicted by characters/strings, while LLMs are designed to\nL1102@P17: interpret and process these as tokens. This creates a complex scenario since the mapping between\nL1103@P17: strings and tokens is intricate and lacks a one-to-one correspondence [22].\nL1104@P17: This section is derived from an earlier blog post4. Readers are also encouraged to read the blog post\nL1105@P17: for additional background and easier understanding.\nL1106@P17: B.1 Implementation Details of Compressed Finite State Machine\nL1107@P17: To simplify the construction of Compressed FSM, we build the original FSM on characters/strings\nL1108@P17: instead of on tokens. We formally define the concepts of singular transition edge and compressed\nL1109@P17: edge as follows:\nL1110@P17: • Singular transition edge: A edge is a singular transition edge if 1) its source node has only one\nL1111@P17: successor node, and 2) there is only one acceptable character/string on it.\nL1112@P17: • Compressed edge: An edge compressed by several consecutively adjacent edges (e0, e1, . . . , ek)\nL1113@P17: if and only if e1, . . . , ek are singular transition edges. The text of the compressed edge is the\nL1114@P17: concatenation of the texts of e0, e1, . . . , ek.\nL1115@P17: Starting from an FSM based on characters, we recursively merge singular transition edges into\nL1116@P17: their preceding ones until further compression is unfeasible, resulting in a Compressed FSM. This\nL1117@P17: approach speeds up the decoding process, as demonstrated by SGLang’s runtime efficiency with the\nL1118@P17: Compressed FSM, shown in Fig. 11.\nL1119@P17: B.2 Handling Tokenization Artifacts with Retokenization\nL1120@P17: When a new token is generated, we get the token’s string and search all the outgoing edges of the\nL1121@P17: current state find the one that starts with the just decoded string, and then move forward. However,\nL1122@P17: when the transition edge is a well-compressed one and contains a very long string, we may anticipate\nL1123@P17: the next rounds’ decoded strings as well. This is where the acceleration happens and we call this\nL1124@P17: process Jump Forward. However, we still need to convert the string into tokens for the next decoding\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLLMLingua: Compressing Prompts for Accelerated Inference of Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2310.05736v1)\nciteturn58view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view8\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 499\nL91: The most similar work to this paper is Selective-Context cite58†Li (2023) , which evaluates the informativeness of lexical units by computing self-information with a small language model, and drops the less informative content for prompt compression. This paper is inspired by Selective-Context and further proposes a coarse-to-fine framework to address its limitations.\nL92: \nL93: cite78†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Framework of the proposed approach LLMLingua.\nL94: ### 2.2 Out-of-Distribution (OoD) Detection\nL95: Recently, a series of studies have been proposed for unsupervised OoD detection. With only in-distribution texts available for learning, these methods either fine-tune a pre-trained language model cite79†Arora et al. (2021) or train a language model from scratch cite80†Mai et al. (2022) . cite81†Wu et al. (2023) analyze the characteristics of these methods and leverage multi-level knowledge distillation to integrate their strengths while mitigating their limitations.\nL96: Finally, perplexity output by the resulting language model is used as the indication of an example being OoD.\nL97: This paper also regards perplexity as a measurement of how well a language model predicts a sample. In contrast to out-of-distribution detection, which identifies examples with high perplexities as indicative of unreliable predictions, we consider tokens with higher perplexity to be more influential during the inference process of language models.\nL98: ### 2.3 LLMs as a Compressor\nL99: \nL100: Recently, many perspectives have interpreted large language models and unsupervised learning as a kind of compressor for world knowledge cite82†Sutskever (2023) ; cite83†Delétang et al. (2023) , by using arithmetic coding cite84†Rissanen (1976) ; cite85†Pasco (1976) . Our research can be viewed as an endeavor to further compress information within prompts by capitalizing on the compression-like characteristics of large language models.\nL351: Our approach holds substantial practical implications, as it not only reduces computational costs but also offers a potential solution for accommodating longer contexts in LLMs. The method of compressing prompts has the potential to enhance downstream task performance by compressing longer prompts and to improve the LLMs’s inference efficiency by compressing the KV cache.\nL352: ## Limitations\nL353: \nL354: There are also some limitations in our approach. For instance, we might observe a notable performance drop when trying to achieve excessively high compression ratios such as 25x-30x on GSM8K, as shown in Figure cite111†3 .\nL355: \nL356: Figure 3: The performance of various prompt compression methods at different compression ratios ($1/\\tau$) on GSM8K. The dashed line corresponds to the Exact Match score obtained from the full-shot prompt.\nL357: It is shown that as the compression ratio increases especially around 25x-30x, all methods as well as ours will experience a substantial performance drop. In comparison with other methods, this performance drop derived from our approach is significantly shifted to much higher compression ratios. We owe this to the Budget Controller and the Iterative Token-level Prompt Compression algorithm, which enable our method to maintain the original prompt information even at some extreme compression ratios.\nL358: The upper limit of the compression ratio for different prompts varies, depending on factors such as prompt length, task type, and the number of sentences involved.\nL359: Additionally, there may be subtle differences between the tokenizers used by the small language model and the black-box LLM, which may result in an underestimation of the prompt’s token length.\n"
